※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=15 diag=none

※ `while` runs its body again for as long as the condition holds.  The
※ condition stands on its own -- there are no parentheses around it, because
※ nothing needs them: what ends it is the body, which begins with a colon or a
※ brace, and neither can be part of an expression.
※
※ It is a statement and not an expression, unlike `if` and `match`.  Those
※ produce a value because every way through them produces one; a loop has a way
※ through that runs the body no times at all, and there is nothing for that way
※ to produce.
※
※ A name a turn changes is the same name on the next turn, which is what makes
※ a loop able to count anything: the value a turn leaves is the value the next
※ turn reads, and the value the last turn leaves is what follows the loop reads.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let total: mut u6 = 0u6
    let n: mut u6 = 5u6
    while n > 0u6:
        total ← total + n
        n ← n - 1u6
    total
